WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.969 --> 00:00:05.890
What’s up guys my name is Evan Kidd.&nbsp; I’m
a filmmaker from North Carolina and I actually

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:10.890
recently just created my debut feature film
Son Of Clowns.&nbsp; It is a ninety-five minute

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:15.770
feature we shot completely independently and
it was filmed with the&nbsp;Canon C100. You know

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:19.189
with filmmaking as a whole I think a lot of
times we get roped into what camera is the

00:00:19.189 --> 00:00:23.189
best or you know what specs can work best
for this film.&nbsp; Every year there’s a brand

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:27.659
new camera and I think for me I really break
a camera down or a choice of camera for any

00:00:27.659 --> 00:00:32.940
film I make based on what’s going to really
push us to the furthest capabilities what

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:37.050
we can do to get me the most time with my
actors. You know I’m a director first and

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:41.480
foremost but I also do DP work as well for
other people’s films so it kind of puts

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:45.510
me in an interesting position to where I do
a lot of corporate DP work, I do narrative

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:49.710
DP work, I just shot a short New Orleans this
past week.&nbsp; It was really interesting because

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:53.550
I actually used the 6D for that but for what
we were trying to go for the director told

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:57.400
me we need to move fast.&nbsp; We have to make
our days, it’s imperative the location we

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.500
were in we gotta be in and out.&nbsp; So for me
it was interesting because I needed the camera

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:07.620
that would really make me as nimble and quick
as possible while also minimizing the setups

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.720
we needed to do.&nbsp; Something that could be
handheld if it needed to be. So I think a

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.570
lot of those decisions are very vital and
key.&nbsp; Coming back to my own film Son Of Clowns

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:20.140
my director of photography Ned Phillips actually
owned the Canon C100 and I really do think

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:24.640
the Canon C100 is probably one of the best
filmmaking tools in terms of camera that you

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.280
can get today as an independent filmmaker.
We thought about renting, we thought about

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:33.210
using my own personal 5D but kinda came back
to what’s gonna give us the highest quality

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:37.580
image because it was a feature we wanted to
really push it a little past the territory

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:42.340
of a DSLR if we could.&nbsp; By no means are we
against it but you know at the same time we

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:47.659
thought about the C100 and C300 line and it
made the most sense because Ned, our DP, owned

00:01:47.659 --> 00:01:51.420
that C100 and he also owned an Atomos Ninja
Recorder.&nbsp; And it’s great because it’s

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.561
actually a monitor that you put on top of
your C100 rig and what that allows you to

00:01:55.561 --> 00:02:00.340
do is not only view your image obviously bigger
as opposed to looking on the viewfinder or

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:06.229
the LCD screen built into the camera but really
it also lets you record flat.&nbsp; A very flat

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:11.680
image which is almost RAW. It doesn’t record
true RAW.&nbsp; It does record Apple ProRes but

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.490
for our intents and purposes that was perfect
because I’m one of those who really like

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:19.959
to get in there.&nbsp; And the editor when it
comes down to it really grade every fine detail.&nbsp;

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:23.319
I love color correction.&nbsp; I love getting
in there and really for us it made the most

00:02:23.319 --> 00:02:27.610
sense because on a budget the camera that
allowed us to move the quickest and to do

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:32.190
the least number of setups and to be on the
go was the C100. And to me that was just an

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:36.849
added treat you know we originally were not
expecting to have that ninja so it really

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:40.700
allowed us to push the image further.&nbsp; What
you’re seeing right now on the screen is

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:47.069
a flat image directly from the ninja camera
on Son Of Clowns.&nbsp; Again this is RAW image

00:02:47.069 --> 00:02:52.650
and now you’re seen the color graded image
and you can see the intricacies and kinda

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:58.630
how far you can really push it. To me honestly
the flat image looks pretty great on it’s

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:04.690
own.&nbsp; A lot better than the digital version
which we also would record as a backup so

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:09.459
basically the way we had our rig was Ned would
record directly into the ninja but we’d

00:03:09.459 --> 00:03:15.780
have a backup of everything we did on the
SD card from the C100.&nbsp; That was not a bad

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:21.590
look at all.&nbsp; But we really enjoyed having
that flat image at our disposal later down

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:26.171
the road in the edit. I’m really inspired
by resource-based filmmaking.&nbsp; Absolutely

00:03:26.171 --> 00:03:31.910
consider myself an independent filmmaker really
on every level and so for me when we came

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:38.920
to looking for a camera it was all about accessibility
and portability.&nbsp; I love the handheld look

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:43.440
so the C100 is great because you know it’s
not huge but it’s also big enough to where

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.530
if you were to hand hold it, if you have an
awesome DP like I did you can basically have

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:53.170
a very good hand-held image that’s also
pretty stable you know you really have some

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:57.000
good weight on that C100&nbsp; between everything
on it especially if you’re throwing that&nbsp;Ninja

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.420
Adapter&nbsp;on it after like we had. So it’s
a great camera for handheld.&nbsp; You don’t

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:06.280
really have to build a whole bunch of shoulder
rigs and steady cams and mounts because in

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:11.440
my opinion if your DP is skilled enough you
will be ok and you’ll get that hand-held

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:16.790
image that’s not like super crazy shaky
or anything like that because again looking

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:21.940
at the images that we captured we were on
the move so much and you know for us it was

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:27.800
really a matter of you know 14+ hour days
I think our longest day was close to 20 hours

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:31.860
it was just absolutely nuts.
We shot Son Of Clowns on a production schedule

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:37.940
of 10 shooting days so the camera had to adapt.&nbsp;
The camera had to be just as game as all of

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:42.001
our crew members.&nbsp; Our crew members were
basically doing the indie film thing where

00:04:42.001 --> 00:04:48.080
they were sleeping on air mattresses, really
accessible locations, trying to cram a lot

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.170
of pages into a day while still you taking
the time we needed.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:56.330
For me my number one priority is really how
can I get as much time devoted to my actors

00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:02.080
as possible and so I knew in the C100 that
it would be a great versatile camera cause

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.270
we wouldn’t have that many tech issues.&nbsp;
We wouldn’t have much freezing or anything

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:11.300
like you might get in a RED or something more
“fancy.”&nbsp; You do want something that

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:16.010
won’t give you a lot of crap along the way.
Filmmaking as they say is hard enough as it

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:21.250
is so why complicate yourself or complicate
matters as a filmmaker.&nbsp; For me again that

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:27.650
C100 really truly resonated well and again
it gelled with the type of flow we were going

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:33.220
for as a film and I think that flow is great
because if you have a camera that’s fighting

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:38.620
the flow of your film.&nbsp; If you’re somehow
getting your hands on a really fancy camera

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:42.960
that takes a lot of time, a lot of set up,
you can’t just run and gun with it.&nbsp; Then

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:48.690
you are going to have to build your days as
a film and has a production to accommodate

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:53.050
that.&nbsp; Because again I don’t want to get
bogged down if my production schedule is only

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:57.539
10 days and we can’t go over.&nbsp; So it was
imperative we made our days and it was also

00:05:57.539 --> 00:06:02.330
imperative that our camera match that mentality.
So just a note for people out there if you

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:06.970
are looking for a 4K camera that is a drawback,
the&nbsp; C100 does not have that.&nbsp; I truly at

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:10.900
this point don’t really care too much for
4K.&nbsp; I think down the road I will but you

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:15.949
know again as a resource-based independent
filmmaker I know my means and I know my level.&nbsp;

00:06:15.949 --> 00:06:19.440
So for me 4K wasn’t really on the table
so you know if you’re looking for that you

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.169
might want to look elsewhere.
But for me to 1080p we did we did manage to

00:06:23.169 --> 00:06:28.450
capture from the image on the C100 with the
Ninja.&nbsp; Apple ProRes again very close to

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:34.810
RAW so seeing that ProRes resolution at 422
was just comparatively great in my opinion.&nbsp;

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:39.280
It gave me what I wanted for an independent
feature and really push the boundaries to

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.750
what we thought that C100 could even do in
the first place. Our big boy lens which we

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:50.520
actually shot probably 90% of the film on
was the&nbsp;Canon 24-105 L Series lens.&nbsp; In

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:55.419
my opinion a great piece of glass, all around
very versatile.&nbsp; I know if I had my choice

00:06:55.419 --> 00:07:00.990
I’d love to have fixed prime lenses for
every single shot but I knew that the way

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:04.720
we were going to shoot would not dictate that.&nbsp;
We needed to be able to zoom.&nbsp; I needed Ned

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:09.280
to have the range he needed quickly and for
him not to be bogged down by switching lenses

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:14.100
with me every 10 seconds if I wanted a&nbsp; different
angle.&nbsp; You know one set-up, one-shot later

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:19.460
so that 24 -105 with it’s zoom really gave
us a crisp and clear image while being a high

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:23.710
enough piece of glass to differentiate it
from other zoom lenses while still giving

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:29.240
us the range and the flexibility to move fast
and keep that production schedule on point.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:33.370
Budget was tight, production days were slim
so we really had to make do with what we had

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:37.810
and I think the fact that we had such a great
cast and crew that really gelled and really

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:42.000
accommodated one another you know from our
camera department down to our actors to everyone

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.630
else on Son of Clowns. Really when it came
down to it we would pool our resources together

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:50.940
and really make the best film that we could
make and I think community and resource-based

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:54.090
filmmaking like that where you know maybe
you don’t have enough money to get a RED

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:58.789
but you know you have the lens and your buddy
who shoots stuff has the body of the camera

00:07:58.789 --> 00:08:02.500
you come together you make a nice little camera
package and that’s basically what we did

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:07.539
and I think if you find dedicated hardworking
passionate individuals who are willing to

00:08:07.539 --> 00:08:11.360
do that with your film, with your camera,
what have you.&nbsp; You really can create the

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:11.890
best film you can and create a great image.

